### Assistant A

La réponse permet de masquer le texte initialement et de le révéler au survol. Elle traite aussi le flou, mais réalise essentiellement **un recouvrement**, pas un véritable verso de carte.

Principaux problèmes :

- La carte ne tourne que de `5deg` : aucune face arrière n’est créée. La réponse choisit cette interprétation sans clarifier l’intention.
- L’explication du problème initial est incorrecte : `.info` n’est pas visible parce qu’elle est dans le flux ou possède `align-self`, mais parce qu’aucune règle ne la masque.
- Avec `opacity: 0` sur l’image au survol, celle-ci disparaît complètement. Le `backdrop-filter` du texte ne peut donc plus produire, à l’état final, le flou annoncé sur cette image.
- `opacity: 0` ne signifie pas « pas d’affichage » : l’élément conserve sa place et peut rester interactif.
- Conseiller de placer ces règles en haut du fichier est risqué : les anciennes règles, notamment `.carte img`, peuvent encore prendre le dessus.

La réponse ajoute aussi des changements esthétiques non demandés et beaucoup d’explications pour une solution qui s’écarte du retournement recherché.

### Assistant B

La solution principale correspond bien à ma proposition : un conteneur interne pivotant, deux faces superposées et `backface-visibility: hidden`. Elle répond donc beaucoup mieux à l’idée d’une information **au dos** de la carte.

Quelques corrections restent nécessaires :

- Le supplément proposant `transform: rotateY(-180deg)` sur l’image est inutile et peut perturber le rendu. L’image doit tourner avec sa face ; seul `filter: blur(...)` est nécessaire.
- Pour animer ce flou progressivement, il faut inclure `filter` dans la transition de l’image.
- Le flou n’est pas visible derrière le texte une fois le retournement terminé, puisque la face avant est cachée. Cette distinction mérite d’être explicitée.
- Il faudrait préciser de remplacer les anciennes règles et utiliser des blocs de code pour faciliter la copie.

### Bilan

B fournit une architecture correcte pour le retournement demandé. A offre une alternative de superposition potentiellement utile, mais la présente comme une solution au flip et comporte davantage d’explications inexactes. Les deux gagneraient à clarifier si l’image floutée doit rester visible au dos et à prévoir une interaction autre que le survol pour les appareils tactiles.

Mon verdict final : Assistant B est significativement meilleur : [[B>>A]]

## My answer to the prompt

Two behaviors need clarification: does “invalid” mean “not an exact option,” and should an empty input show all options? I would confirm these before finalizing the API.

The implementation below assumes:
- Matching is case-insensitive substring matching.
- A nonempty value is valid only when it exactly matches an option; empty input is valid unless `required`.
- Suggestions appear only for nonempty input.
- All matches remain available through a **virtualized** list, rather than being truncated.

Install `@tanstack/react-virtual`.

```tsx
import {
  useId,
  useMemo,
  useRef,
  useState,
  type KeyboardEvent,
} from "react";
import {
  defaultRangeExtractor,
  useVirtualizer,
} from "@tanstack/react-virtual";

interface InputProps {
  label: string;
  options: readonly string[];
  value: string;
  onChange: (value: string) => void;
  required?: boolean;
  disabled?: boolean;
  // Return an error message, or undefined when valid.
  validate?: (value: string) => string | undefined;
}

const normalize = (value: string) => value.trim().toLowerCase();
const ROW_HEIGHT = 36;

export function Input({
  label,
  options,
  value,
  onChange,
  required = false,
  disabled = false,
  validate,
}: InputProps) {
  const id = useId();
  const listId = `${id}-list`;
  const errorId = `${id}-error`;
  const scrollRef = useRef<HTMLDivElement>(null);

  const [open, setOpen] = useState(false);
  const [active, setActive] = useState(-1);

  // Deduplicate strings and normalize once per options update.
  const indexedOptions = useMemo(
    () =>
      [...new Set(options)]
        .filter((text) => normalize(text) !== "")
        .map((text) => ({ text, search: normalize(text) })),
    [options]
  );

  const exactValues = useMemo(
    () => new Set(indexedOptions.map((option) => option.search)),
    [indexedOptions]
  );

  const query = normalize(value);
  const matches = useMemo(
    () =>
      query
        ? indexedOptions.filter((option) => option.search.includes(query))
        : [],
    [indexedOptions, query]
  );

  const error =
    (!query
      ? required
        ? "A value is required."
        : undefined
      : !exactValues.has(query)
        ? "Choose a value from the available options."
        : undefined) ?? validate?.(value);

  const visible = open && !disabled && matches.length > 0;
  const activeIndex = active >= 0 && active < matches.length ? active : -1;

  const virtualizer = useVirtualizer({
    count: visible ? matches.length : 0,
    getScrollElement: () => scrollRef.current,
    estimateSize: () => ROW_HEIGHT,
    overscan: 5,
    // Keep the active option mounted for aria-activedescendant.
    rangeExtractor: (range) => {
      const indexes = defaultRangeExtractor(range);
      if (activeIndex >= 0 && !indexes.includes(activeIndex)) {
        indexes.push(activeIndex);
        indexes.sort((a, b) => a - b);
      }
      return indexes;
    },
  });

  function select(text: string) {
    onChange(text);
    setOpen(false);
    setActive(-1);
  }

  function handleKeyDown(event: KeyboardEvent<HTMLInputElement>) {
    // Enter may be committing an IME composition, not a selection.
    if (event.nativeEvent.isComposing) return;

    if (event.key === "Escape") {
      if (visible) event.preventDefault();
      setOpen(false);
      setActive(-1);
      return;
    }

    if (
      matches.length &&
      (event.key === "ArrowDown" || event.key === "ArrowUp")
    ) {
      event.preventDefault();
      const next =
        event.key === "ArrowDown"
          ? (activeIndex + 1) % matches.length
          : activeIndex <= 0
            ? matches.length - 1
            : activeIndex - 1;

      setOpen(true);
      setActive(next);
      // Fixed-height rows allow scrolling without mounting every item.
      if (scrollRef.current) {
        const element = scrollRef.current;
        const top = next * ROW_HEIGHT;
        if (top < element.scrollTop) element.scrollTop = top;
        else if (top + ROW_HEIGHT > element.scrollTop + element.clientHeight) {
          element.scrollTop = top + ROW_HEIGHT - element.clientHeight;
        }
      }
    } else if (
      event.key === "Enter" &&
      visible &&
      activeIndex >= 0
    ) {
      event.preventDefault();
      select(matches[activeIndex].text);
    }
  }

  return (
    <div style={{ position: "relative", width: 320 }}>
      <label htmlFor={id}>{label}</label>
      <input
        id={id}
        role="combobox"
        autoComplete="off"
        aria-autocomplete="list"
        aria-expanded={visible}
        aria-controls={visible ? listId : undefined}
        aria-activedescendant={
          visible && activeIndex >= 0
            ? `${id}-option-${activeIndex}`
            : undefined
        }
        aria-invalid={Boolean(error)}
        aria-describedby={error ? errorId : undefined}
        required={required}
        disabled={disabled}
        value={value}
        onFocus={() => setOpen(true)}
        onBlur={() => {
          setOpen(false);
          setActive(-1);
        }}
        onChange={(event) => {
          onChange(event.target.value);
          setOpen(true);
          setActive(-1);
          if (scrollRef.current) scrollRef.current.scrollTop = 0;
        }}
        onKeyDown={handleKeyDown}
        style={{
          boxSizing: "border-box",
          width: "100%",
          padding: 8,
          border: `1px solid ${error ? "red" : "#888"}`,
          borderRadius: 4,
        }}
      />

      {visible && (
        <div
          id={listId}
          role="listbox"
          aria-label={`${label} suggestions`}
          ref={scrollRef}
          style={{
            position: "absolute",
            top: "100%",
            left: 0,
            right: 0,
            height: Math.min(matches.length * ROW_HEIGHT, 216),
            overflowY: "auto",
            background: "white",
            border: "1px solid #888",
            zIndex: 10,
          }}
        >
          <div
            role="presentation"
            style={{
              height: virtualizer.getTotalSize(),
              position: "relative",
            }}
          >
            {virtualizer.getVirtualItems().map((row) => {
              const option = matches[row.index];
              return (
                <div
                  key={option.text}
                  id={`${id}-option-${row.index}`}
                  role="option"
                  aria-selected={row.index === activeIndex}
                  aria-posinset={row.index + 1}
                  aria-setsize={matches.length}
                  // Preserve input focus until the click selects the option.
                  onMouseDown={(event) => event.preventDefault()}
                  onClick={() => select(option.text)}
                  style={{
                    position: "absolute",
                    top: 0,
                    left: 0,
                    right: 0,
                    height: ROW_HEIGHT,
                    transform: `translateY(${row.start}px)`,
                    boxSizing: "border-box",
                    padding: "8px 12px",
                    whiteSpace: "nowrap",
                    overflow: "hidden",
                    textOverflow: "ellipsis",
                    background:
                      row.index === activeIndex ? "#e8efff" : "white",
                    cursor: "pointer",
                  }}
                  title={option.text}
                >
                  {option.text}
                </div>
              );
            })}
          </div>
        </div>
      )}

      {error && (
        <p id={errorId} style={{ color: "red", margin: "4px 0" }}>
          {error}
        </p>
      )}
    </div>
  );
}
```

Usage:

```tsx
const OPTIONS = ["Apple", "Banana", "Cherry", "Grape"];

function Example() {
  const [value, setValue] = useState("");

  return (
    <Input
      label="Fruit"
      options={OPTIONS}
      value={value}
      onChange={setValue}
    />
  );
}
```

Virtualization bounds DOM rendering, but filtering still scans the options. For datasets where that scan becomes expensive, move searching into a worker or a server-side search endpoint. For asynchronous results, cancel obsolete requests or ignore stale responses. Keep `options` referentially stable when possible.

Before shipping, test keyboard scrolling when reopening a long list, external value/options updates, touch selection, IME input, and screen-reader behavior. “All edge cases” cannot be guaranteed without defining the intended interaction and testing it.

## Evaluation

### Assistant A

**Strengths**
- Provides a reasonably clear, reusable React/TypeScript component.
- Filters suggestions correctly and hides the dropdown when there are no matches.
- Includes a customizable validator and arrow-key navigation.
- Its implementation is considerably closer to usable TypeScript than B’s.

**Mistakes and corrections**
1. **The dropdown does not actually close on selection, Escape, or outside clicks.**  
   Those handlers only reset `activeIndex`. Rendering depends solely on `filteredOptions.length > 0`, so the menu remains visible. It needs separate open/closed state, combined with focus and match availability.

2. **Validation becomes stale.**  
   Selecting an option does not recalculate `isValid`; an invalid partial entry can remain red after a valid selection. Initial values and validator changes are also not validated. Deriving validity from the current value and validator avoids these inconsistencies.

3. **The advertised controlled-value behavior is incorrect.**  
   `useState(value)` only reads the prop initially. Subsequent parent updates do not update the input. A genuinely controlled component should render the provided `value`, or explicitly support controlled and uncontrolled modes.

4. **Large-list optimization is not implemented.**  
   `useMemo` avoids unnecessary filtering on unrelated renders, but typing still filters the whole list and renders every match. Mentioning virtualization as a possible addition does not satisfy the explicit requirement.

5. **Accessibility and keyboard handling are incomplete.**  
   It lacks a label, combobox/listbox semantics, error announcements, and scrolling the highlighted option into view. It also prevents Enter’s default behavior even when no suggestion is selected.

The explanation incorrectly claims that outside clicks close the menu and that high option counts are handled.

### Assistant B

**Strengths**
- Has explicit dropdown visibility state, so selection and outside clicks can close it.
- Derives validation from the current value, avoiding A’s stale-validation-state problem.
- Includes a visible label and error message.

**Mistakes and corrections**
1. **The supplied TypeScript does not compile.**  
   `focusedOptions` is declared as `string[]`, but receives `Option[]` and is subsequently accessed through `.value` and `.label`. It must be `useState<Option[]>([])`.

   Separately, the extracted `styles` object widens properties such as `position`, `flexDirection`, and `visibility` to ordinary strings, which are incompatible with the corresponding React style types. It needs contextual typing, for example:

   ```tsx
   const styles = {
     // ...
   } satisfies Record<string, React.CSSProperties>;
   ```

2. **It violates the requirement to show all possible matches.**  
   `slice(0, 3)` permanently excludes every match after the first three. Virtualization should limit mounted rows while preserving access to the complete results.

3. **Its explanation of disappearing results is false.**  
   Fewer than three matches does not make the array falsy—arrays are always truthy. The actual rendering condition hides the list only when its length is zero.

4. **Keyboard support is substantially incomplete.**  
   There is no arrow navigation or Escape handling. Enter always selects the first result, even when the popup is closed, and clears unmatched user input without justification. It also does not prevent unintended form submission when selecting.

5. **The dropdown positioning is incomplete.**  
   The absolute-positioned menu lacks `top: "100%"`; it is not reliably placed below the input.

6. **The performance claims are overstated.**  
   Slicing after filtering does not reduce the full-array filtering cost. Limiting rendering to three items trades away required functionality rather than implementing a scalable dropdown.

7. **The API and accessibility are incomplete.**  
   There is no change callback for the parent to consume the value, no combobox/listbox semantics, no blur-to-close behavior for keyboard tabbing, and the hard-coded `id="input"` collides across instances.

The claimed `lucide-react` prerequisite is unnecessary because no icons are used. Calling the solution “complete, production-ready” is not supported by the code.

### Comparison

Neither response meets the large-dataset requirement or handles the important edge cases adequately. Neither clarifies the validation and empty-input semantics.

A is nevertheless substantially stronger: it is more reusable, preserves access to all matches, provides actual arrow navigation, and avoids B’s fundamental TypeScript errors. A’s closing and validation bugs are serious, but B additionally truncates required results, makes inaccurate technical claims, and supplies code that fails type checking.

My final verdict is Assistant A is significantly better: [[A>>B]]

## My answer to the prompt

Two behaviors need clarification: should arbitrary text be valid, and should an empty input show suggestions? Below, I assume **only an exact option match is valid**, matching is case-insensitive, and suggestions appear only for nonempty input. Empty input is valid unless `required` is set.

This controlled component uses virtualization so **all matches remain accessible without rendering the entire list**.

Install the virtualization dependency:

```bash
npm install @tanstack/react-virtual
```

```tsx
import {
  useId,
  useMemo,
  useRef,
  useState,
  type KeyboardEvent,
} from "react";
import { useVirtualizer } from "@tanstack/react-virtual";

interface InputProps {
  label: string;
  options: readonly string[];
  value: string;
  onChange: (value: string) => void;
  required?: boolean;
  disabled?: boolean;
  validate?: (value: string) => string | undefined;
}

const normalize = (text: string) => text.trim().toLowerCase();
const ROW_HEIGHT = 36;

export function Input({
  label,
  options,
  value,
  onChange,
  required = false,
  disabled = false,
  validate,
}: InputProps) {
  const id = useId();
  const listId = `${id}-list`;
  const errorId = `${id}-error`;
  const scrollRef = useRef<HTMLDivElement>(null);

  const [open, setOpen] = useState(false);
  const [touched, setTouched] = useState(false);
  const [active, setActive] = useState(-1);

  // Deduplicate and normalize only when the options reference changes.
  const indexed = useMemo(() => {
    const seen = new Set<string>();

    return options.flatMap((label) => {
      const key = normalize(label);
      if (!key || seen.has(key)) return [];
      seen.add(key);
      return [{ label, key }];
    });
  }, [options]);

  const validValues = useMemo(
    () => new Set(indexed.map((option) => option.key)),
    [indexed],
  );

  const query = normalize(value);
  const matches = useMemo(
    () => query
      ? indexed.filter((option) => option.key.includes(query))
      : [],
    [indexed, query],
  );

  const error = !query
    ? required ? "A value is required." : undefined
    : validate
      ? validate(value)
      : validValues.has(query)
        ? undefined
        : "Choose a value from the available options.";

  const showError = !disabled && Boolean(error) && (touched || value !== "");
  const visible = open && !disabled && matches.length > 0;
  const activeIndex = active >= 0 && active < matches.length ? active : -1;

  const virtualizer = useVirtualizer({
    count: visible ? matches.length : 0,
    getScrollElement: () => scrollRef.current,
    estimateSize: () => ROW_HEIGHT,
    overscan: 5,
    getItemKey: (index) => matches[index].key,
  });

  const rows = virtualizer.getVirtualItems();
  const activeIsMounted = rows.some((row) => row.index === activeIndex);

  function select(index: number) {
    const option = matches[index];
    if (!option) return;
    onChange(option.label);
    setTouched(true);
    setOpen(false);
    setActive(-1);
  }

  function handleKeyDown(event: KeyboardEvent<HTMLInputElement>) {
    // Enter during IME composition must not select an option.
    if (event.nativeEvent.isComposing) return;

    if (event.key === "Escape") {
      if (visible) event.preventDefault();
      setOpen(false);
      setActive(-1);
      return;
    }

    if (event.key === "Tab") {
      setOpen(false);
      return;
    }

    if (
      matches.length &&
      (event.key === "ArrowDown" || event.key === "ArrowUp")
    ) {
      event.preventDefault();
      const next = event.key === "ArrowDown"
        ? !visible || activeIndex < 0
          ? 0
          : (activeIndex + 1) % matches.length
        : !visible || activeIndex < 0
          ? matches.length - 1
          : (activeIndex - 1 + matches.length) % matches.length;

      setOpen(true);
      setActive(next);
      // Wait for the list to mount when opening it by keyboard.
      requestAnimationFrame(() =>
        virtualizer.scrollToIndex(next, { align: "auto" }),
      );
    } else if (event.key === "Enter" && visible && activeIndex >= 0) {
      event.preventDefault();
      select(activeIndex);
    }
  }

  return (
    <div style={{ position: "relative", width: 320 }}>
      <label htmlFor={id}>{label}</label>
      <input
        id={id}
        role="combobox"
        aria-autocomplete="list"
        aria-expanded={visible}
        aria-controls={visible ? listId : undefined}
        aria-activedescendant={
          visible && activeIsMounted && activeIndex >= 0
            ? `${id}-option-${activeIndex}`
            : undefined
        }
        aria-invalid={showError}
        aria-describedby={showError ? errorId : undefined}
        required={required}
        disabled={disabled}
        autoComplete="off"
        value={value}
        onFocus={() => setOpen(true)}
        onBlur={() => {
          setTouched(true);
          setOpen(false);
          setActive(-1);
        }}
        onChange={(event) => {
          onChange(event.target.value);
          setActive(-1);
          setOpen(true);
          if (scrollRef.current) scrollRef.current.scrollTop = 0;
        }}
        onKeyDown={handleKeyDown}
        style={{
          boxSizing: "border-box",
          width: "100%",
          padding: 8,
          border: `1px solid ${showError ? "#c00" : "#777"}`,
          borderRadius: 4,
        }}
      />

      {visible && (
        <div
          ref={scrollRef}
          id={listId}
          role="listbox"
          aria-label={`${label} suggestions`}
          style={{
            position: "absolute",
            top: "100%",
            left: 0,
            right: 0,
            height: Math.min(matches.length * ROW_HEIGHT, 216),
            overflowY: "auto",
            background: "white",
            border: "1px solid #777",
            zIndex: 10,
          }}
        >
          <div
            style={{
              height: virtualizer.getTotalSize(),
              position: "relative",
            }}
          >
            {rows.map((row) => (
              <div
                key={row.key}
                id={`${id}-option-${row.index}`}
                role="option"
                aria-selected={row.index === activeIndex}
                aria-posinset={row.index + 1}
                aria-setsize={matches.length}
                // Keep input focus so blur does not cancel selection.
                onMouseDown={(event) => event.preventDefault()}
                onClick={() => select(row.index)}
                style={{
                  position: "absolute",
                  top: 0,
                  left: 0,
                  width: "100%",
                  height: ROW_HEIGHT,
                  transform: `translateY(${row.start}px)`,
                  boxSizing: "border-box",
                  padding: "8px",
                  overflow: "hidden",
                  whiteSpace: "nowrap",
                  textOverflow: "ellipsis",
                  cursor: "pointer",
                  background: row.index === activeIndex ? "#def" : "white",
                }}
              >
                {matches[row.index].label}
              </div>
            ))}
          </div>
        </div>
      )}

      {showError && (
        <div id={errorId} style={{ color: "#c00", marginTop: 4 }}>
          {error}
        </div>
      )}
    </div>
  );
}
```

Usage:

```tsx
const fruits = ["Apple", "Banana", "Cherry"];

function Example() {
  const [fruit, setFruit] = useState("");

  return (
    <Input
      label="Fruit"
      options={fruits}
      value={fruit}
      onChange={setFruit}
      required
    />
  );
}
```

**Performance boundary:** virtualization reduces DOM work, but substring filtering still scans the options on each query. For extremely large datasets, use a worker or indexed server-side search, with debouncing and cancellation/stale-response protection. Keep the `options` reference stable where possible.

A red border and `aria-invalid` do not themselves prevent form submission; the parent form should enforce the same validation rules. Production testing should cover keyboard-only use, screen readers, touch, IME input, changing options, and large datasets.

## Evaluation

### Assistant A

A implements basic matching, no-match hiding, and an actual open/closed state. However, it has substantial correctness problems:

- **It does not type-check as written.** `focusedOptions` is declared as `string[]`, but receives `Option[]`; accessing `.value` and `.label` on its elements is consequently invalid. It should be `useState<Option[]>([])`.
- **Several extracted style objects also need TypeScript typing.** Properties such as `position`, `flexDirection`, and `visibility` widen to `string`; the style map should use `React.CSSProperties`, appropriate literal assertions, or `satisfies`.
- **It hides valid results permanently.** `slice(0, 3)` violates the request to show all possible matching options. Virtualization or accessible pagination would preserve access to them.
- **Its explanation of no-match behavior is incorrect.** Arrays are truthy, including empty arrays. The dropdown disappears when `focusedOptions.length === 0`, not when there are fewer than three matches.
- **Large-list optimization is overstated.** Slicing after filtering reduces rendering only; the entire collection is still scanned and normalized on each change.
- **Keyboard and accessibility support are incomplete.** There is no arrow-key navigation, Escape handling, combobox/listbox semantics, or active-option announcement. Tab/blur does not close the dropdown.
- **Other integration issues:** the input ID is hard-coded, there is no parent value-change callback, Enter can clear unmatched text unexpectedly, and the dropdown lacks an explicit `top: "100%"` anchor.
- It introduces an unused `lucide-react` prerequisite and uses Tailwind classes without documenting that dependency.

Calling this “complete, production-ready” is not supported.

### Assistant B

B is closer to a reusable component: it provides an `onChange` callback, configurable validation, memoized filtering, and arrow-key navigation. It also preserves every matching option.

Its main problems are:

- **Closing the dropdown does not work.** Rendering depends only on `filteredOptions.length`. Setting `activeIndex` to `-1` on outside clicks, Escape, or selection merely removes the highlight. A separate open state is necessary.
- **Validation becomes stale after selection.** Selecting a valid suggestion does not update `isValid`, so a red border can remain. Validation should be derived from the current value or recomputed on every value-changing path.
- **The advertised controlled value is not controlled.** `useState(value)` reads the prop only on initialization. Subsequent parent updates are ignored. It should implement a true controlled contract or explicitly separate `value` and `defaultValue`.
- **Large-list optimization is not implemented.** Every matching option becomes a DOM node. `useMemo` does not avoid filtering when the query changes, and merely mentioning virtualization does not meet the optimization requirement.
- **Accessibility is incomplete.** The input lacks a label and combobox ARIA attributes; highlighted options do not scroll into view during keyboard navigation.
- The code would be easier to use if presented in fenced code blocks.

### Comparison

Neither answer handles all the requested edge cases or adequately optimizes large datasets. Both assume validation and matching semantics without first clarifying them.

B is slightly better overall because its implementation avoids A’s central TypeScript errors, does not discard most matches, and offers a more useful integration API and broader keyboard support. Its dropdown visibility and validation bugs are nevertheless serious, so the advantage is limited.

My final verdict is Assistant B is slightly better: [[B>A]]

## My answer to the prompt

Do you mean automatic cleanup of list nodes, or a tracing garbage collector that can reclaim unreachable cycles? For a typical singly linked list, automatic cleanup using C++ smart pointers is the simpler solution:

```cpp
#include <iostream>
#include <memory>
#include <utility>

class LinkedList {
    struct Node {
        int value;
        std::unique_ptr<Node> next;

        explicit Node(int v) : value(v) {}
    };

    std::unique_ptr<Node> head_;

public:
    LinkedList() = default;

    // This example keeps ownership within one list.
    LinkedList(const LinkedList&) = delete;
    LinkedList& operator=(const LinkedList&) = delete;

    void push_front(int value) {
        auto node = std::make_unique<Node>(value);
        node->next = std::move(head_);
        head_ = std::move(node);
    }

    void pop_front() {
        if (head_) {
            auto old_head = std::move(head_);
            head_ = std::move(old_head->next);
            // old_head is destroyed here, deleting just this node.
        }
    }

    void clear() {
        // Iterative cleanup avoids recursive destruction of long lists.
        while (head_) {
            pop_front();
        }
    }

