    <article aria-label="The origins of the Romanians">
      <p>Romanian is a Romance language in a neighborhood where most languages are not, a fact that has supplied historians with a genuine puzzle and nationalists with an unusually durable talking point. Its descent from spoken Latin is not seriously disputed; the difficult questions concern where its speakers’ ancestors lived, how they moved, and when their language became recognizably Romanian. Rome conquered much of Dacia under Trajan in 106 CE, establishing a province that covered only part of present-day Romania, principally within and around the Carpathian arc. Under Aurelian, around 271–275, the empire withdrew its administration and army south of the Danube, although that decision does not establish that every provincial inhabitant departed. Nor did Roman government disappear simultaneously from all territory now Romanian: Dobruja, on the lower Danube and Black Sea, remained within the empire much longer. Asking whether there was a “continuous Roman presence” therefore requires separating three things that are too often bundled together: imperial government, a Latin-speaking population, and the much later emergence of a Romanian identity.</p>

      <p>The Daco-Roman continuity hypothesis holds that Romanized communities survived north of the Danube after the imperial withdrawal and contributed substantially to the formation of the Romanians there. Its strongest versions place considerable weight on rural survival, with populations adapting to successive political masters rather than obligingly evacuating whenever the frontier changed. Archaeologists have identified post-withdrawal occupation at some former Roman sites, alongside coins, objects, and Christian material that indicate continuing connections with the Roman world. Such discoveries make a uniformly empty landscape difficult to defend, but an occupied settlement does not arrive with a certificate identifying its inhabitants’ language. Likewise, Romanian’s inherited Latin vocabulary establishes linguistic descent without, by itself, locating every stage of that descent in Transylvania or elsewhere north of the river. Continuity is consequently a serious historical proposition, but the evidence for continued habitation is firmer than any attempt to trace an uninterrupted, securely identified Romanian-speaking community through every intervening century.</p>

      <p>The principal rival is not simply that migrants mixed with local people, something virtually every plausible account accepts, but that Romanian took shape mainly south of the Danube before its speakers moved north. Often called the immigrationist theory, this interpretation was influentially systematized by the nineteenth-century historian Robert Roesler, whose account placed Romanian settlement in Transylvania relatively late. Its supporters have emphasized the evacuation of Roman Dacia, the scarcity of unambiguous early written references to Romanians north of the river, and linguistic connections with the Balkans. Romanian shares vocabulary with Albanian whose explanation remains debated, while its substantial Slavic inheritance and features shared with other Balkan languages attest to prolonged regional contact. These clues matter, but they are not precise coordinates: the historical distribution of languages was different from today’s, and contact can occur through movement in more than one direction. Medieval references to “Vlachs,” an external designation often applied to Eastern Romance speakers, must also be read in context rather than treated as a census of a modern nation.</p>

      <p>A third family of explanations treats the Danube less as an ethnic dividing line than as a corridor across which related populations repeatedly moved. On this account, Romanian developed within a broader zone of Romanized communities on both sides of the river, with local survival, migration, and sustained contact contributing in proportions that remain contested. The existence of other Eastern Romance languages, including Aromanian, supports a wider Balkan setting, although it does not settle where the ancestors of today’s Romanian speakers lived at any particular date. Slavic settlement and interaction profoundly shaped vocabulary and regional culture, while later political and ecclesiastical connections added further layers without displacing the language’s Latin grammatical foundations. This approach accommodates population mixture without confusing it with linguistic replacement, and continuity without imagining communities sealed off from their neighbors for a millennium. Its appeal is that it fits the workings of borderlands; its difficulty is that a flexible regional model still needs evidence for particular places, movements, and periods.</p>

      <p>The dispute acquired exceptional political force in Transylvania, where historical arguments became entangled with unequal political rights under Habsburg rule and, later, competing Romanian and Hungarian national claims. From the late eighteenth century, intellectuals associated with the Transylvanian School emphasized Roman ancestry and antiquity of settlement as arguments for Romanian recognition and equality. Some Hungarian and other immigrationist writers, conversely, used claims of later Romanian arrival to challenge Romanian assertions of historical precedence, especially in relation to the medieval Hungarian kingdom. Neither political association automatically proves or disproves a historical hypothesis, but both help explain why gaps in the evidence have so often been filled with remarkable confidence. Under Nicolae Ceaușescu, Romanian national communism promoted deep indigenous roots, Dacian importance, continuity, and unity, making approved antiquity an instrument of state legitimacy. The essential distinction is that the date of a population’s arrival may be a historical question, but it is not a sound basis for deciding the rights of people living there now.</p>

      <p>A careful conclusion begins with what is secure: Romanian descends from Latin, its formation belongs to the Romanized world of southeastern Europe, and its speakers’ history includes extensive contact with other populations. The precise balance between survival north of the Danube and movement from the south remains harder to establish, especially because the early medieval documentary record is sparse and uneven. Archaeology can illuminate settlement and exchange, historical linguistics can reconstruct contact and change, and ancient DNA can sometimes identify ancestry shifts, but none can simply translate a burial into a spoken language or a national identity. Strong claims of either complete evacuation or an unchanged population surviving in place outrun what those methods can demonstrate. The most useful question is therefore not whether Romanians are “really Roman,” but how Latin-speaking communities became Romanian across a changing landscape of empires, migrations, and medieval societies. History offers a complicated inheritance here, not a title deed—and is more interesting for the distinction.</p>
