## My answer to the prompt

The main cost is storing **26 pointers for every node**, plus a separate allocation and bookkeeping for each node’s `vector`.

Two useful improvements are:

- **Small change:** replace `vector<Node*>` with `array<Node*, 26>`. This removes the child-vector allocation and metadata, but still stores 26 pointers per node.
- **Larger improvement:** store nodes in a pool and use **integer indices instead of pointers**. On typical 64-bit systems, 26 pointers require 208 bytes, while 26 32-bit indices require 104 bytes.

Here is the second approach, preserving your interface and using iterative traversal:

```cpp
#include <array>
#include <string>
#include <vector>

class Solution {
    struct Node {
        std::array<int, 26> child;
        int cnt = 0;

        Node() {
            child.fill(-1);
        }
    };

public:
    std::vector<int> sumPrefixScores(std::vector<std::string>& words) {
        std::vector<Node> nodes(1);  // Root is index 0.

        for (const std::string& word : words) {
            int current = 0;

            for (char ch : word) {
                int c = ch - 'a';
                int next = nodes[current].child[c];

                if (next == -1) {
                    next = static_cast<int>(nodes.size());
                    nodes.emplace_back();

                    // Access by index after emplace_back:
                    // vector growth may invalidate pointers/references.
                    nodes[current].child[c] = next;
                }

                current = next;
                ++nodes[current].cnt;
            }
        }

        std::vector<int> answer;
        answer.reserve(words.size());

        for (const std::string& word : words) {
            int current = 0;
            int score = 0;

            for (char ch : word) {
                current = nodes[current].child[ch - 'a'];
                score += nodes[current].cnt;
            }

            answer.push_back(score);
        }

        return answer;
    }
};
```

This assumes lowercase `a`–`z`, and that node indices, counts, and scores fit in `int`, as your original code does. Every queried word was inserted, so its path exists.

Other memory benefits:

- No separate allocation for each node.
- No recursive call-stack usage.
- No string copies in the range loops.
- All trie storage is automatically released; your original code never deletes its nodes.

A pooled node typically occupies **108 bytes**, excluding vector spare capacity. Vector growth can temporarily increase peak memory. Reserving `1 + sum(word lengths)` nodes avoids growth, but can waste substantial memory when words share prefixes. If most nodes have very few children, a sparse edge representation can reduce memory further.

