## My answer to the prompt

Use your example’s format: **a sequence of `{ index, value }` pairs, each containing two signed 16-bit integers**. Define the byte order explicitly as **little-endian**, rather than transmitting native memory layouts.

For the full controller state, each UDP datagram contains **18 pairs × 4 bytes = 72 bytes**, with no header.

| Offset within each pair | Field | Encoding |
|---|---|---|
| 0–1 | Event index | Little-endian `i16`, valid range 0–17 |
| 2–3 | Event value | Little-endian `i16` |

### Zig client changes

Keep your enum, initialization, keyboard handling, and socket creation. Replace `sendControllerState` with:

```zig
const EVENT_COUNT = @intFromEnum(ControllerEvents.START) + 1;
const PACKET_SIZE = EVENT_COUNT * 4;

fn sendControllerState(
    sock: posix.fd_t,
    addr: *const net.Address,
    controllerState: []const i16,
) !void {
    if (controllerState.len != EVENT_COUNT) {
        return error.InvalidControllerState;
    }

    var packet: [PACKET_SIZE]u8 = undefined;

    for (controllerState, 0..) |value, i| {
        const offset = i * 4;
        const index: i16 = @intCast(i);

        std.mem.writeInt(
            i16,
            packet[offset..][0..2],
            index,
            .little,
        );
        std.mem.writeInt(
            i16,
            packet[offset + 2..][0..2],
            value,
            .little,
        );
    }

    const sent = try posix.sendto(
        sock,
        packet[0..],
        0,
        &addr.any,
        addr.getOsSockLen(),
    );

    if (sent != packet.len) {
        return error.ShortDatagramSend;
    }
}
```

This uses the modern Zig syntax already suggested by your original code. Your existing call remains valid:

