A straightforward loop makes the accumulation easier to read. Preallocating the vectors can also avoid reallocations:

```rust
let capacity = readings.len();
let mut times = Vec::with_capacity(capacity);
let mut cpu_temperature = Vec::with_capacity(capacity);
let mut cpu_usage = Vec::with_capacity(capacity);
let mut memory_usage = Vec::with_capacity(capacity);

for reading in readings.iter() {
    if let (Some(timestamp), Some(conditions)) =
        (reading.timestamp.as_ref(), reading.condition.as_ref())
    {
        times.push(TimeHelper::to_offset_date_time(timestamp));
        cpu_temperature.push(conditions.cpu_temperature);
        cpu_usage.push(conditions.cpu_usage);
        memory_usage.push(conditions.memory_usage);
    }
}
```

This preserves the original behavior: a reading is skipped unless **both** fields are present, and the four output vectors remain aligned and ordered.

The main potential performance improvement is preallocation, not replacing iterator adapters: Rust often optimizes iterator chains into code comparable to a manual loop. Reserving `readings.len()` may waste memory if most readings are skipped; use a smaller estimate or `Vec::new()` in that case. Benchmark representative data to confirm any speedup.

