## My answer to the prompt

Use **word-at-a-time scanning**: read a `size_t`, test whether it contains a zero byte, and only inspect individual bytes when that test succeeds.

For 8-bit bytes, the zero-byte test is:

```c
(x - ones) & ~x & highs
```

where `ones` has `0x01` in every byte and `highs` has `0x80` in every byte.

Here is an illustrative implementation:

```c
#include <stddef.h>
#include <stdint.h>
#include <string.h>
#include <limits.h>

_Static_assert(CHAR_BIT == 8, "Requires 8-bit bytes");

/*
 * Platform-specific assumptions:
 * - uintptr_t is available and addresses have conventional alignment.
 * - size_t has no padding bits.
 * - Every word loaded lies entirely within readable allocated storage,
 *   including the word containing the terminating NUL.
 *
 * That last condition is stronger than ordinary strlen's contract.
 */
size_t fast_strlen(const char *s)
{
    const unsigned char *p = (const unsigned char *)s;
    const size_t ones = (size_t)-1 / 255;
    const size_t highs = ones * 128;
    size_t n = 0;

    /* Align the address before scanning words. */
    while ((uintptr_t)p % sizeof(size_t) != 0) {
        if (*p == 0)
            return n;
        ++p;
        ++n;
    }

    for (;;) {
        size_t x;

        /* Avoid alignment and strict-aliasing violations in the load. */
        memcpy(&x, p, sizeof x);

        if ((x - ones) & ~x & highs) {
            for (size_t i = 0; i < sizeof x; ++i) {
                if (p[i] == 0)
                    return n + i;
            }
        }

        p += sizeof x;
        n += sizeof x;
    }
}
```

This works regardless of endianness because the final scan follows memory order. Compilers commonly turn the fixed-size `memcpy` into a word load.

**Important limitation:** A word load may read past the terminating NUL. Even if that does not cross an unmapped page, reading outside the allocated object is not generally valid standard C. `memcpy` avoids aliasing problems but does not fix out-of-bounds reads. A general-purpose library implementation needs platform/compiler-specific guarantees; for ordinary application code, prefer the implementation’s optimized `strlen`. Benchmark before assuming a custom version is faster.

