### 6. Lock dependencies and build

Nix’s experimental features are not enabled globally until the first successful rebuild. Bootstrap them using command-line options:

```bash
cd /etc/nixos

sudo nix --extra-experimental-features "nix-command flakes" flake lock

sudo nixos-rebuild build \
  --flake /etc/nixos#workstation \
  --option experimental-features "nix-command flakes"
```

`flake.lock` pins the exact input revisions. Keep it alongside your configuration.

If `/etc/nixos` is already a Git repository, make sure the new files are tracked before building:

```bash
sudo git -C /etc/nixos add flake.nix flake.lock desktop.nix home.nix
```

Git-backed flakes do not include untracked files.

If the build reports conflicting settings, inspect the existing `configuration.nix` and remove the duplicate or conflicting desktop/audio declarations. Do not delete installation-specific settings to make errors disappear.

### 7. Activate and reboot

Once the build succeeds:

```bash
sudo nixos-rebuild switch \
  --flake /etc/nixos#workstation \
  --option experimental-features "nix-command flakes"

sudo reboot
```

Save your work before switching desktop services.

At GDM, select your user. If there is a session selector, choose **GNOME**, not **GNOME on Xorg**.

### 8. Verify the result

Inside GNOME, open a terminal:

```bash
echo "$XDG_SESSION_TYPE"
nvim --version
code --version
curl --version
git --version
```

The session type should be:

```text
wayland
```

Launch Zed, Brave, and Bitwarden from the application overview. They are installed but **not automatically started**.

Log into Bitwarden interactively. Install its browser extension in Brave separately if you want browser autofill.

### 9. Rebuild, update, and recover

After editing configuration:

```bash
sudo nixos-rebuild switch --flake /etc/nixos#workstation
```

To update pinned dependencies:

```bash
cd /etc/nixos
sudo nix flake update
sudo nixos-rebuild build --flake /etc/nixos#workstation
sudo nixos-rebuild switch --flake /etc/nixos#workstation
```

For a release upgrade, change both release branches together and read the release notes. Do not automatically increase either state-version value.

To return to the previous system generation:

```bash
sudo nixos-rebuild switch --rollback
```

If the new system cannot boot or display a login screen, select an older generation in the boot menu. Restore previous configuration files and `flake.lock` before rebuilding; rollback does not revert your source files or all application data.

