Do you mean **serialize the contents into bytes**, or **marshal the object to a specific native API**? The latter requires the native API’s expected layout.

A `HashSet<string>` cannot be copied directly into a self-contained byte array: it contains references to other managed objects, and its internal layout is runtime-dependent. For a compact binary representation of its contents, you can define your own format:

```csharp
using System;
using System.Collections.Generic;
using System.IO;
using System.Text;

public static class HashSetBytes
{
    // Format (little-endian):
    // Int32: item count
    // For each item:
    //   Int32: UTF-8 byte length (-1 means null)
    //   Byte[length]: UTF-8 data
    public static byte[] Serialize(HashSet<string> set)
    {
        if (set == null)
            throw new ArgumentNullException(nameof(set));

        // Throw on invalid UTF-16 instead of silently replacing characters.
        var utf8 = new UTF8Encoding(false, true);

        using var stream = new MemoryStream();
        using var writer = new BinaryWriter(stream, utf8, leaveOpen: true);

        writer.Write(set.Count);

        foreach (string item in set)
        {
            if (item == null)
            {
                writer.Write(-1);
                continue;
            }

            byte[] data = utf8.GetBytes(item);
            writer.Write(data.Length);
            writer.Write(data);
        }

        writer.Flush();
        return stream.ToArray();
    }
}
```

Usage:

```csharp
var set = new HashSet<string> { "apple", "banana", "" };
byte[] bytes = HashSetBytes.Serialize(set);
```

Important limitations:
- Do not modify the set while serializing it.
- Enumeration order is not guaranteed; sort a snapshot first if you require deterministic bytes.
- This stores contents, not the comparer or internal hash-table layout. Reconstruction should use the appropriate comparer, such as `StringComparer.OrdinalIgnoreCase`, when applicable.
- A decoder should validate counts and lengths and reject truncated input.
- This UTF-8 format rejects strings containing unpaired UTF-16 surrogates. If preserving every possible .NET string is required, store raw UTF-16 code units instead.

