## My answer to the prompt

Extract the HTTP operations into an **API client**, put the dependent API calls in a **provisioning service (Facade)**, and expose the provisioned user through a **test-scoped Playwright fixture**. Keep browser interactions in the existing **Page Object**.

This separates responsibilities without introducing unnecessary factories or builders.

The example below assumes:
- Creation returns `{ id }`, and permissions return `{ username, password }`, as in your example.
- Permission requests use the field `id`, not `userId`.
- A deletion endpoint exists at `DELETE /users/v2/:id` and returns `204`. Replace this with your actual API contract.
- Playwright’s `baseURL` is configured. Payload fields, UI routes, and selectors are illustrative.

### 1. API contract and implementation

```typescript
// users-api.ts
import type { APIRequestContext, APIResponse } from '@playwright/test';

export interface CreateUserInput {
  username: string;
  email: string;
  password: string;
}

export interface PermissionsInput {
  role: 'admin' | 'editor' | 'reader';
}

export interface Credentials {
  username: string;
  password: string;
}

export interface CreatedUser {
  id: string;
}

// Narrow interface: provisioning depends on capabilities, not HTTP details.
export interface UsersApi {
  create(input: CreateUserInput): Promise<CreatedUser>;
  setPermissions(
    id: string,
    input: PermissionsInput
  ): Promise<Credentials>;
  delete(id: string): Promise<void>;
}

async function requireStatus(
  response: APIResponse,
  expected: number,
  operation: string
): Promise<void> {
  if (response.status() !== expected) {
    throw new Error(
      `${operation}: expected HTTP ${expected}, got ${response.status()}`
    );
  }
}

export class PlaywrightUsersApi implements UsersApi {
  constructor(private readonly request: APIRequestContext) {}

  async create(input: CreateUserInput): Promise<CreatedUser> {
    const response = await this.request.post('/users/v2/create', {
      data: input,
    });
    await requireStatus(response, 201, 'Create user');

    const body = await response.json();
    if (typeof body?.id !== 'string' || body.id.length === 0) {
      throw new Error('Create user returned an invalid id');
    }

    return { id: body.id };
  }

  async setPermissions(
    id: string,
    input: PermissionsInput
  ): Promise<Credentials> {
    const response = await this.request.post('/users/v2/permissions', {
      data: { ...input, id },
    });
    await requireStatus(response, 200, 'Set user permissions');

    const body = await response.json();
    if (
      typeof body?.username !== 'string' ||
      body.username.length === 0 ||
      typeof body?.password !== 'string' ||
      body.password.length === 0
    ) {
      throw new Error('Permissions response returned invalid credentials');
    }

    return { username: body.username, password: body.password };
  }

  async delete(id: string): Promise<void> {
    const response = await this.request.delete(
      `/users/v2/${encodeURIComponent(id)}`
    );

    // Permit already-deleted resources, e.g. when testing user deletion.
    if (response.status() !== 404) {
      await requireStatus(response, 204, 'Delete user');
    }
  }
}
```

Playwright sends JSON payloads through **`{ data: payload }`**, not by passing the payload directly as the second argument.

### 2. Provisioning service

The service owns the multi-call workflow and tracks resources immediately after creation, so cleanup also works when a later permissions call fails.

```typescript
// user-provisioner.ts
import type {
  CreateUserInput,
  Credentials,
  PermissionsInput,
  UsersApi,
} from './users-api';

export interface ProvisionedUser extends Credentials {
  id: string;
}

export class UserProvisioner {
  private readonly createdIds: string[] = [];

  constructor(private readonly users: UsersApi) {}

  async provision(
    input: CreateUserInput,
    permissions: PermissionsInput
  ): Promise<ProvisionedUser> {
    const { id } = await this.users.create(input);
    this.createdIds.push(id);

    const credentials = await this.users.setPermissions(id, permissions);
    return { id, ...credentials };
  }

  async cleanup(): Promise<void> {
    const failures: string[] = [];

    // Attempt every deletion even if one fails.
    for (const id of [...this.createdIds].reverse()) {
      try {
        await this.users.delete(id);
      } catch (error) {
        failures.push(
          `${id}: ${error instanceof Error ? error.message : String(error)}`
        );
      }
    }

    this.createdIds.length = 0;

    if (failures.length > 0) {
      throw new Error(`User cleanup failed:\n${failures.join('\n')}`);
    }
  }
}
```

