## My solution

The original order matters: we cannot necessarily keep the largest elements.

Let
\[
m=\left\lfloor\frac{n-1}{k}\right\rfloor,\qquad r=n-mk.
\]
Exactly \(m\) operations are performed, leaving \(r\) elements.

### 1. Characterize possible remaining arrays

Suppose the surviving elements originally occupied positions
\[
i_1<i_2<\dots<i_r.
\]

The deleted elements before, between, and after these positions must form gaps whose lengths are multiples of \(k\). This is necessary because no deletion can cross an element that survives. It is also sufficient: simply delete each gap in blocks of size \(k\).

Consequently, the surviving positions have exactly the form
\[
i_j=j+t_jk,\qquad
0\le t_1\le t_2\le\dots\le t_r\le m.
\]

### 2. Check a candidate median

For a candidate \(x\), call an element good if it is at least \(x\).

The lower median of \(r\) elements is at least \(x\) precisely when at least
\[
\left\lfloor\frac r2\right\rfloor+1
\]
of those elements are good.

We can maximize the number of good surviving elements using dynamic programming.

Process \(t=0,1,\dots,m\). Let `dp[j]` be the maximum number of good elements among the first \(j\) survivors using choices of \(t_j\) no larger than the current \(t\).

For each \(t\), process \(j=1,2,\dots,r\):
```text
dp[j] = max(dp[j], dp[j-1] + (a[t*k+j] >= x))
```

The existing `dp[j]` skips the current candidate; the second option chooses position \(tk+j\). Updating \(j\) in increasing order allows consecutive survivors to use the same \(t\).

Each check visits
\[
(m+1)r\le mk+r=n
\]
states, so it takes \(O(n)\) time.

Binary search finds the largest feasible \(x\).

### C++17 code

```cpp
#include <bits/stdc++.h>
using namespace std;

int main() {
    ios::sync_with_stdio(false);
    cin.tie(nullptr);

    int T;
    cin >> T;

    while (T--) {
        int n, k;
        cin >> n >> k;

        vector<int> a(n);
        for (int &v : a) cin >> v;

        int m = (n - 1) / k;
        int r = n - m * k;
        int need = r / 2 + 1;

        auto feasible = [&](int x) {
            const int NEG = -1000000000;
            vector<int> dp(r + 1, NEG);
            dp[0] = 0;

            for (int t = 0; t <= m; ++t) {
                for (int j = 1; j <= r; ++j) {
                    int good = (a[t * k + j - 1] >= x);
                    dp[j] = max(dp[j], dp[j - 1] + good);
                }
            }

            return dp[r] >= need;
        };

        int lo = *min_element(a.begin(), a.end());
        int hi = *max_element(a.begin(), a.end());

        while (lo < hi) {
            int mid = lo + (hi - lo + 1) / 2;
            if (feasible(mid)) {
                lo = mid;
            } else {
                hi = mid - 1;
            }
        }

        cout << lo << '\n';
    }
}
```

**Complexity:** \(O(n\log V)\) time per test case, where \(V\le10^9\), and \(O(n)\) total auxiliary storage including the input.

