## My answer

The cost equals the sum of prefix balances: each matched pair contributes `1` to the balance at every position between its opening and closing brackets.

For any valid restoration:
- After each odd position, the balance is odd and nonnegative, so it is at least `1`.
- After an even position containing `'('`, the balance is at least `2`.
- After an even position containing `')'`, the balance is at least `0`.

These minimum balances can all be achieved: at each missing position, place `'('` if the current balance is zero; otherwise, place `')'`. The balance after every odd position becomes `1`, and after every even position it becomes either `0` or `2`. The last fixed bracket must be `')'` because a valid restoration exists, so the resulting sequence is regular.

Therefore:

\[
\text{minimum cost}=\frac n2+2\cdot\text{(number of fixed opening brackets)}.
\]

```cpp
#include <bits/stdc++.h>
using namespace std;

int main() {
    ios::sync_with_stdio(false);
    cin.tie(nullptr);

    int t;
    cin >> t;

    while (t--) {
        int n;
        string s;
        cin >> n >> s;

        long long answer = n / 2;
        for (int i = 1; i < n; i += 2) {
            if (s[i] == '(') answer += 2;
        }

        cout << answer << '\n';
    }
}
```

**Complexity:** `O(n)` time per test case and `O(1)` auxiliary space, excluding the input string.

